> On Mar 20, 2018, at 18:45, John Mattsson <john.mattsson@ericsson.com> =
wrote:
>=20
> Correct, I just copied pasted the length of the arrays, should be =
length =3D cid_length + encrypted_record.length.
>=20
> The example was taken from draft-ietf-tls-tls13-27. If I understand =
correctly, It seems like the same circular definition is done there as =
well....
>=20
> -----------------------------------------------------
>      struct {
>          ContentType opaque_type =3D application_data; /* 23 */
>          ProtocolVersion legacy_record_version =3D 0x0303; /* TLS v1.2 =
*/
>          uint16 length;
>          opaque encrypted_record[TLSCiphertext.length];
>      } TLSCiphertext;
> -----------------------------------------------------
>=20
> Shouldn't this be...
>=20
> -----------------------------------------------------
>      struct {
>          ContentType opaque_type =3D application_data; /* 23 */
>          ProtocolVersion legacy_record_version =3D 0x0303; /* TLS v1.2 =
*/
>          uint16 length;
>          opaque encrypted_record[encrypted_record.length];
>      } TLSCiphertext;
> -----------------------------------------------------

There is no field called encrypted_record.length. TLSCiphertext.length =
refers to the length field in the TLSCiphertext struct rather than the =
length of the TLSCiphertext itself (which is a few bytes longer). See =
Section 3, Presentation Language, for the precise definitions.

Steve

>=20
> Does this mean the=20
> =EF=BB=BFOn 2018-03-20, 19:29, "Stephen Checkoway" <s@pahtak.org> =
wrote:
>=20
>=20
>=20
>> On Mar 20, 2018, at 11:38, John Mattsson <john.mattsson@ericsson.com> =
wrote:
>>=20
>> I think Connection ID is an important enabler for end-to-end security =
with (D)TLS. There seems to be important use cases for connection ID in =
TLS as well, see https://www.ietf.org/mailman/listinfo/atlas. At the =
Monday afternoon TLS session, it was stated that Connection ID in TLS =
was unemployable in the wild due to middleboxes. Couldn't that be solved =
by placing the cid field after the length field? E.g.
>>=20
>>  struct {
>>     ContentType opaque_type =3D application_data; /* 23 */
>>     ProtocolVersion legacy_record_version =3D 0x0303; /* TLS v1.2 */
>>     uint16 length;
>>     opaque cid[cid_length];               // New field
>>     opaque encrypted_record[TLSCiphertext.length];
>>  } TLSCiphertext;
>>=20
>>  length  The sum of cid_length and TLSCiphertext.length
>=20
>    This defines length in terms of itself since length is =
TLSCiphertext.length.
>=20
>    --=20
>    Stephen Checkoway
>=20
>=20
>=20
>=20
>=20
>=20
>=20

--=20
Stephen Checkoway



